7xo4|v|To cause to stop or to rest after motion; hence, to deposit; to lay down; to lodge; to reposit. ; To lay at rest; to cause to be calm or quiet; to compose; to rest, -- often reflexive; as, to repose one's self on a couch. ; To place, have, or rest; to set; to intrust. ; To lie at rest; to rest. ; Figuratively, to remain or abide restfully without anxiety or alarms. ; To lie; to be supported; as, trap reposing on sand. ; A lying at rest; sleep; rest; quiet. ; Rest of mind; tranquillity; freedom from uneasiness; also, a composed manner or deportment. ; A rest; a pause. ; That harmony or moderation which affords rest for the eye; -- opposed to the scattering and division of a subject into too many unconnected parts, and also to anything which is overstrained; as, a painting may want repose.	o:86t9,95,3;35nv,55,3;2l27,21,3;91ol,10,1~86t9,95,3;2l27,21,3;35nv,55,3;91ol,10,1	s:3mcx,103,17;148b,43,1~3mcx,103,17;148b,43,1	d:3mcx,86,5~3mcx,86,5
